#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158295} :activity/id #uuid "0c713b1b-8699-5cdf-acd5-178d556abfff" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048495 :topic/id #uuid "a4841cae-d95f-5e07-960c-2030a42a2568" :topic/math-academy-id 2975} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158295 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158564 :tutorial/content "Recall that $f(x)$ is an **even function** if\n\n$$\nf(-x) = f(x).\n$$\n\nThe graph of an even function is always symmetric about the $y$-axis.\n\n![](images/fc/fc57d05c6bef23243df97979c0680054c6ec66b836a97871970ee055c60eab4a.png)\n\nAlso recall that $g(x)$ is an **odd function** if\n\n$$\ng(-x) = -g(x).\n$$\n\nThe graph of an odd function always has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. In other words, if we rotate the graph $180^\\circ$ about the origin, the resulting graph looks the same.\n\n![](images/d2/d21ae289b641f26e3b43dc7035e2ea02147d44dc490f0ab5e6e34381ea4c2db3.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "e9042a5a-4bbc-5b00-aec4-0982199b1ab9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6806 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "33e51af5-883a-5d53-a82e-9983b0aab23d" :step/math-academy-id 41319 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158316}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158296 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157396 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156997 :question/id #uuid "9cfff4db-e33e-5a3c-a1d4-99e910f2c2fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-10897"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2688742c-1655-557a-888d-af7e2179a89c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048563} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049873}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157952} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354651} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386771} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394806} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394813}] :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Even Functions"} :step/id #uuid "9dffa825-7698-5068-9cf9-029959145880" :step/math-academy-id 41320 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158297}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158297 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158565 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the odd function $f(x)$ shown below.\n\n![](images/7c/7c179ee67a04113130e65e3ce4b8581c969724c380fbe903423d023427d573f8.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 0\n$$\n\nbecause the positive and negative areas cancel each other out.\n\nFurthermore, by the adjacent intervals rule, we can write\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^0 f(x)  \\, \\textrm dx + \\int_{0}^a f(x)  \\, \\textrm dx = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^0 f(x)  \\, \\textrm dx = - \\int_{0}^a f(x)  \\, \\textrm dx.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9707c582-25f4-5558-b5b5-e6af4f83c071" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6807 :tutorial/title "Integrals of Odd Functions"} :step/id #uuid "a69a25f0-35d2-5e6f-99dc-f9e17ed11afa" :step/math-academy-id 41323 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158298}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158298 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157397 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156998 :question/id #uuid "a3e0d847-4dd1-5a47-948c-06959065b73f" :question/math-academy-id "e-10898"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354655} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354656} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413341} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413343}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero"} :step/id #uuid "3dd1c1c0-5948-50f9-802e-942f886fd7e6" :step/math-academy-id 41324 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158299}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158299 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157398 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156999 :question/id #uuid "29ca6f83-ceb9-54fa-b8c4-6c0fbbd4b742" :question/math-academy-id "e-10911"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebbaf66e-6155-510f-b4d8-e38dd4c4f05c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157607} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275339} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275340} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275341} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308602} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308603} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308604} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308605} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308606} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308607} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413344}] :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Odd Functions"} :step/id #uuid "3288d02d-a5b0-5d8a-8137-f96ae6e90b23" :step/math-academy-id 41371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158316 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158566 :tutorial/content "Let's consider our even function $f(x)$ once more.\n\n![](images/7a/7a1b83234d7d028484eb939146db822a087ebffa063ff74dcd3b73478acd0399.png)\n\nWhen we have a definite integral of an even function, we can often use symmetry to simplify it. \n\nFrom the picture above, we have that\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^0 f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = \\int_{0}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = A.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =A+A \\\\  & =2A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo summarize, if $f(x)$ is even, then\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx  = 2\\int_{0}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx .\n$$\n\nThis rule is helpful because definite integrals where one of the integration limits is zero are often easier to evaluate." :tutorial/id #uuid "340dd64d-312a-5516-b425-9cf0b512e446" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6850 :tutorial/title "Integrals of Even Functions"} :step/id #uuid "2b925bf3-52e2-5fd3-a231-2bd9005b1e1c" :step/math-academy-id 41640 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158317}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158317 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158567 :tutorial/content "Throughout this lesson, it's worth remembering that the basic trigonometric functions can be classified as even or odd. \n\n- $\\cos x$ is an even function:\n\n$$\n\\cos(-x) = \\cos x\n$$\n\nThis function is symmetric about the $y$-axis.\n\n![](images/68/684c18a8cbd2aa9cf478ff5a569add34a7157eea2fed7075a56a2cee2aec985f.png)\n\n- $\\sin x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\sin(-x) = -\\sin x\n$$\n\nThis function has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin.\n\n![](images/91/911c92dd5f3d6924226b49f2f25aeba67c8dca676612ccf2830e3a15871a49d6.png)\n\n- $\\tan x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\tan(-x) = -\\tan x\n$$\n\nThis function has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin.\n\n![](images/92/92e670859be89f70e0c8199a7b368e187ab30fd3682478832690ee842595b62f.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "6163a166-7434-5d31-846e-676e81407a7d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6851 :tutorial/title "Trigonometric Functions"} :step/id #uuid "55d52e16-03f0-5165-836c-65302f301bf0" :step/math-academy-id 41641 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158296}}] :activity/title "Evaluating Definite Integrals Using Symmetry" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157523}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413348} :activity/id #uuid "dd7f20dd-b98f-586f-b688-7ce1c3a853b4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048495 :topic/id #uuid "a4841cae-d95f-5e07-960c-2030a42a2568" :topic/math-academy-id 2975} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413345 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275341 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a4bbb7d-18d6-5b61-997d-b85518d9f085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{A}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272893} {:answer/id #uuid "947e1701-dda4-539d-abc1-fe98fe7dc964" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273680} {:answer/id #uuid "9e50ecf2-3ff8-5c23-8e90-43e354e46ca2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273752} {:answer/id #uuid "d85bad73-fa5d-5455-afb4-82cf9a4293cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274279} {:answer/id #uuid "fff717bf-fcc8-5e40-9aff-2c2401a6f86d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274636}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186274279} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5db26d1-9e5a-5da7-a51d-01407ba0e210" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275037}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c649e814-a4ed-51c9-ad8d-f8d4317c4c2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-156188" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{0}^{5}\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x=A,$ what is the value of ${\\int }_{-5}^{0}\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{(-x)}{{e}^{(-x)}+{e}^{-(-x)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-x)}{{e}^{-x}+{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{{e}^{-x}+{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{5}\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the adjacent intervals rule for integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{5}\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-5}^{0}\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{5}\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-5}^{0}\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x+A & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-5}^{0}\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0277a93a-ee40-523e-aca9-461457efed21" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413346}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413346 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413344 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad199620-f508-5a84-91ee-7b219e7a24f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413334}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413334} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd5de39b-4f47-50b7-a647-9365f8fad97f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c1148328-1d0d-53fb-826c-7f5db78f1f65" :question/math-academy-id "q-334355" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{3}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry.\n\n${\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{3}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x),$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x).$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)={x}^{3}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{3}\\sqrt{4-(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)={x}^{3}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{3}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5efb4e1f-5f25-58cc-8cad-df223dc54867" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413349}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413347 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413343 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "130e6afa-dce0-5b08-819c-ef94704234fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413325} {:answer/id #uuid "376dd84b-186c-5a4f-8a0a-85f3f4cc251e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413326} {:answer/id #uuid "5a0f7606-ed30-5617-8e4d-36a130ebe24b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413328} {:answer/id #uuid "761e54dd-814f-565c-bb2b-669fb57d2836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413329} {:answer/id #uuid "f5c2246d-16ff-5312-93dd-63dfdff73262" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413336}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413328} :answer-field/id #uuid "93e076d5-a5ae-573d-9e84-8303573a1f36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "93763c90-6504-59a6-ad5e-8ed17cc5ac6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-115272" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt[3]{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-4}^{4}x{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)={e}^{2x}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & ={e}^{2(-x)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x} \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is an odd function, but integral II is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\n- The function $h(x)=x{e}^{{x}^{2}}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x){e}^{(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & =-x{e}^{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{4}x{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "8d2569ba-9a65-57c0-8435-59f5608e5eec" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413345}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413348 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413341 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47aae119-de52-5a6f-b7a1-ed5cfb5a10e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413327} {:answer/id #uuid "7c9326e8-8412-5f0f-be79-a57ba80e957f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413330} {:answer/id #uuid "a3cfc328-4fc1-5747-b490-dfadf05a9a5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413332} {:answer/id #uuid "a66e2889-63d9-58f5-b599-fac84f95bb5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413333} {:answer/id #uuid "df465832-9958-51d8-a73c-05aadf6adf21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413335}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413332} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e7444ba-e9b0-5dc3-868c-f11ef347b50d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0c87590b-cc07-5050-b79f-9ad013757661" :question/math-academy-id "q-88693" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-6}^{6}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-x(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)={x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-g(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "96c22ae1-3ee6-5184-a661-9475acf9100c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413347}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413349 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413342 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8284560a-94dd-597a-81d2-57c5cccc574d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-p" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413331}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413331} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b770ccd-99d1-5c98-a430-d44955178ce6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413338}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7e16dfe4-483e-56b2-ae34-2f1386783053" :question/math-academy-id "q-334371" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x=p,$ express the value of ${\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x$ in terms of $p.$\n\n${\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x),$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x).$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x)}{{e}^{-x}+{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the adjacent intervals rule for integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x+p & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-p.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "bb566f75-c1c5-5c01-ad23-23adc24970e3"}] :activity/title "Evaluating Definite Integrals Using Symmetry" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413324}]}
